5. When Should We Use if?

Use if when the condition is more complicated.

For example:

function Page() {
  const status = "loading";


  if (status === "loading") {
    return <h1>Loading...</h1>;
  }


  if (status === "error") {
    return <h1>Something went wrong.</h1>;
  }


  return <h1>Data loaded!</h1>;
}

This is much easier to read than creating a giant nested ternary.

6. Second Method — Ternary Operator

The ternary operator is extremely common in React.

Syntax:

condition ? trueValue : falseValue

Think:

             condition
                 │
          ┌──────┴──────┐
          ↓             ↓
        TRUE          FALSE
          ↓             ↓
     trueValue      falseValue

Example:

function App() {
  const isLoggedIn = true;


  return (
    <div>
      {isLoggedIn ? <h1>Welcome</h1> : <h1>Please Login</h1>}
    </div>
  );
}
7. Understand Every Part

Look at:

{isLoggedIn ? <h1>Welcome</h1> : <h1>Please Login</h1>}
{ }

We are inside JSX.

JavaScript expressions go inside {}.

isLoggedIn

Our condition.

?

Means:

If true, use the next value.

<h1>Welcome</h1>

Displayed if condition is true.

:

Means:

Otherwise.

<h1>Please Login</h1>

Displayed if condition is false.

8. Ternary Example With State

Now let's make it interactive.

import { useState } from "react";


function App() {
  const [isLoggedIn, setIsLoggedIn] = useState(false);


  return (
    <div>
      {isLoggedIn ? (
        <h1>Welcome, Ithesh!</h1>
      ) : (
        <h1>Please Login</h1>
      )}


      <button onClick={() => setIsLoggedIn(!isLoggedIn)}>
        {isLoggedIn ? "Logout" : "Login"}
      </button>
    </div>
  );
}


export default App;

This is a very useful example.

