12. How && Works

JavaScript evaluates:

true && something

Result:

something

But:

false && something

doesn't produce the desired JSX.

Therefore:

{isAdmin && <button>Admin Panel</button>}

means:

If isAdmin is true, display the button.

13. Example
function Dashboard() {
  const isAdmin = true;


  return (
    <div>
      <h1>Dashboard</h1>


      {isAdmin && <button>Admin Panel</button>}
    </div>
  );
}

Output:

Dashboard


[Admin Panel]

Change:

const isAdmin = false;

Output:

Dashboard

The Admin Panel disappears.

14. Ternary vs &&

This is an important distinction.

Ternary

Use when you have two possible UI outcomes.

{isLoggedIn ? <Dashboard /> : <Login />}

Meaning:

true  → Dashboard
false → Login
&&

Use when you only want to show something when true.

{isAdmin && <AdminPanel />}

Meaning:

true  → AdminPanel
false → nothing
