20. Production Example

Imagine your application fetches products from a backend.

Your state might eventually be:

const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);
const [error, setError] = useState(null);
const [products, setProducts] = useState([]);

Then:

if (loading) {
  return <LoadingSpinner />;
}


if (error) {
  return <ErrorMessage />;
}


if (products.length === 0) {
  return <EmptyProducts />;
}


return <ProductList products={products} />;

Now your UI handles four different situations:

             Product Page
                  │
                  ↓
               Loading?
              /        \
            YES         NO
            ↓            ↓
         Spinner       Error?
                       /     \
                     YES      NO
                     ↓         ↓
                   Error     Products?
                              /      \
                            EMPTY    HAVE DATA
                              ↓         ↓
                           Empty      List

This is a very real industry pattern.

21. Conditional Rendering With Components

You don't have to put everything inside one component.

For example:

function Login() {
  return <h1>Please Login</h1>;
}


function Dashboard() {
  return <h1>Dashboard</h1>;
}


function App() {
  const isLoggedIn = true;


  return (
    <div>
      {isLoggedIn ? <Dashboard /> : <Login />}
    </div>
  );
}

This is better than putting a huge amount of JSX inside App.

Why?

Because each component has a clear responsibility.

App
 │
 ├── Dashboard
 │
 └── Login
22. Important React Concept

Remember this sentence:

State determines what UI React should display.

For example:

State:
isLoggedIn = false


        ↓


UI:
<Login />

Then:

State:
isLoggedIn = true


        ↓


UI:
<Dashboard />

React is essentially responding to state.

